.TH KI 1 "2026-07-24" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ki \- Knowledge Islands command-line interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ki
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B ki help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B ki completions
.I shell
.br
.B ki diag
.br
.B ki doctor
.br
.B ki version
.br
.B ki bootstrap
.RI [ --refresh ]
.br
.B ki dev on
.I local-harness-path
.br
.B ki dev off
.br
.B ki acquire chatgpt import
.I capture-directory
.B --output
.I kep-directory
.RI [ --dry-run ]
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ki
is the Knowledge Islands command-line interface.
It provides stable, local-first commands for working with Knowledge Islands
material.
This page describes the current unreleased development surface and distinguishes
it from planned commands.
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
Commands prefixed with
.B *
document the intended interface.
They are not available in this build, do not appear in
.B ki help
or completion output, and may change before release.
.SS Repository maintenance
.TP
.B ki repo audit [--repo \fIpath\fR] [--skill \fIskill\fR]
Run the native read-only operations registered by the selected repository's
declared capabilities. The host verifies every selected harness inventory and
integrity-covered operation before importing it in process; it never dispatches
repository-vendored runners.
.TP
.B ki repo conform [--repo \fIpath\fR] [--skill \fIskill\fR] [--dry-run]
Plan and apply the same capabilities' safe writes, then re-audit. A dry run
validates every complete write set and reports the affected paths without
changing the repository. Publication refuses unsafe, changed, duplicate, or
symbolic-link targets.
.TP
.B *ki repo educate [--repo \fIpath\fR] [--skill \fIskill\fR]
Explain the selected repository's declared capabilities and their recommended
maintenance actions without changing repository state.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B help [command]
Print root or command-specific help.
.TP
.B --help, -h
Print root help.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the CLI version.
.TP
.B version
Print the CLI version.
.TP
.B completions \fIshell\fR
Print Bash or Zsh completion source to standard output.
.TP
.B diag
Print the CLI version, installation mode, resolved paths, and user
configuration without changing state.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the user configuration, configured agent skill directories, installed
harnesses, and configured KI user-skill links without changing state.
.TP
.B *ki docs
Open or print the appropriate KI documentation location.
.SS General maintenance
.TP
.B ki bootstrap
Create the user-managed KI environment configuration from detected supported
agents, install the built-in verified canonical harness, then install the core
user skills —
.BR ki-bootstrap ,
.BR ki-delegate ,
.BR ki-next ,
.BR ki-plan ,
and
.BR ki-recap
— into every configured agent's user skill directory. An existing agent
configuration is reused without replacement. Pass
.B --refresh
to redetect every supported runtime and rebuild the user configuration's
agents, installed harnesses, and KI-managed user skill links.
.TP
.B *ki update
Update the executable and refresh installed harnesses.
.TP
.B *ki upgrade
Apply newer capability releases in the resolved repository context.
.TP
.B *ki missing|outdated
Report capabilities absent from the selected scope or with a newer verified
latest release available.
.TP
.B *ki search|cleanup
Search the available capability set or remove managed stale state.
.SS Harness management
.TP
.B ki harness info \fIharness-id\fR [--json]
Inspect one user-installed compatible harness and its capability
inventory without changing user state.
.TP
.B ki harness list
List compatible harnesses already installed.
.TP
.B ki harness install \fIharness-id\fR
Install the built-in canonical harness or one configured, verified compatible
harness from immutable release evidence. Bootstrap itself accepts only the
canonical identifier. A successful installation records the harness identifier
in the user configuration's
.B [harnesses].ids
array.
.TP
.B ki harness uninstall \fIharness-id\fR [--dry-run]
Remove one non-base harness with only recognised payload directories. The base harness
cannot be removed; a dry run verifies that a non-base harness can be removed without
changing user state. A successful removal deletes its identifier from
.BR [harnesses].ids .
.SS Skills management
.TP
.B *ki skill repo add|remove \fIharness-id:skill-name\fR
Add or remove a registered skill in one selected repository.
.TP
.B *ki skill user add|remove \fIharness-id:skill-name\fR
Add or remove a registered skill in the selected user runtime.
.SS Acquisition
.TP
.B acquire chatgpt import \fIcapture-directory\fR --output \fIkep-directory\fR [--dry-run] [--json]
Validate a user-prepared local ChatGPT capture and create a deterministic
Knowledge Export Package (KEP).
The command does not control a browser, contact ChatGPT, read credentials,
discover a repository, or extract reusable knowledge.
.SS Development
.TP
.B ki dev on \fIlocal-harness-path\fR
Switch the canonical harness to a local checkout for development. The checkout
must contain the core bootstrap skill at
.IR skills/keystone/ki-bootstrap/SKILL.md
and physical
.IR skills ,
.IR agents ,
and
.IR hooks
payload directories. KI replaces only the canonical harness's managed payload
directories with links to that checkout, records the local path in
.BR [local].path ,
and repoints the five core user skills.
.TP
.B ki dev off
Remove that recognised local payload projection, reinstall the verified
canonical archive, clear
.BR [local].path ,
and repoint the five core user skills to the restored archive.
.SH PATHS
Each KI path uses the first non-empty value:
.nf
$KI_DATA_HOME   | $XDG_DATA_HOME/ki   | ~/.local/share/ki
$KI_CONFIG_HOME | $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ki | ~/.config/ki
$KI_CACHE_HOME  | $XDG_CACHE_HOME/ki  | ~/.cache/ki
$KI_STATE_HOME  | $XDG_STATE_HOME/ki  | ~/.local/state/ki
.fi
Run
.B ki diag
to inspect the effective values.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
Homebrew installs a released regular executable.
From a checkout, run
.B bun run build
then
.B ./install.sh --copy
to install a regular standalone executable for the current platform.
For development against this checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to place a symbolic link in
.B KI_CLI_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin ).
The linked command executes the current TypeScript source through Bun without
changing its release version, and links the manual into
.B KI_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the corresponding
.IR share/man/man1
directory).
Use a dedicated development command directory or invoke
.B ./bin/ki
directly when comparing it with Homebrew's installed executable. The linked
source mode requires Bun; a regular compiled executable does not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operational check or requested operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command grammar was invalid.
